<Docs
  title="Reviewing code on another machine — GitWarren over SSH"
  description="Add a machine you can already reach over ssh and GitWarren installs itself there over the same connection. The host needs nothing but git."
  eyebrow="Another machine"
  heading="Reviewing code that is somewhere else."
  intro="A repository lives on one machine, and so does its review. GitWarren does not copy either one — it reaches the machine the code is already on, runs the review there, and renders it in the window in front of you."
>
  <h2>What actually happens</h2>

  <p>
    Nothing is replicated and nothing is synced. The window you are looking at
    is a view onto a host; git is read on the host, SQLite lives on the host,
    and the agent that works on that code talks to the GitWarren next to it
    rather than across a network.
  </p>

  <p>
    That means a machine which is switched off is shown as offline, not as
    whatever it last said. It also means there is no merge conflict to have, no
    replication to fall behind, and nothing of yours sitting on a server.
  </p>

  <h2>Adding a host</h2>

  <p>
    Open <strong>Other machines</strong> and add one. There are three ways to
    reach a machine, and the form asks for whichever the carrier needs:
  </p>

  <div class="table-scroll">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Kind</th><th>You give it</th><th>Good for</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>SSH</td>
          <td><code>user@machine</code></td>
          <td>A VPS, a build box, a PC — anything with an ssh daemon.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Tailnet</td>
          <td><code>pc-wsl</code></td>
          <td>
            A machine on your tailnet that is listening. See <a
              href="/docs/tailnet">the tailnet guide</a>.
          </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>WSL</td>
          <td>a distro, picked from a list</td>
          <td>
            Windows only. See <a href="/docs/wsl">WSL, from the Windows app</a>.
          </td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <p>
    For SSH, the only prerequisite is that <code>ssh user@machine</code> already
    works from a terminal without asking you anything. GitWarren does not manage
    keys, agents or config — it uses the ssh you already have, with the setup
    you already trust.
  </p>

  <h2>The install</h2>

  <p>
    A machine added this way has GitWarren installed on it over the same
    connection. <strong>The host needs nothing but git.</strong> The daemon is
    shipped as a self-contained tarball with a Node binary inside it, so there
    is no runtime to install first and nothing on the host that can upgrade out
    from under it.
  </p>

  <p>
    The tarball is a release asset. The machine you are sitting at fetches it
    and streams it down the pipe — a couple of seconds on a normal connection —
    unpacks it under <code>~/.gitwarren/</code>, and writes the same{" "}
    <code>~/.gitwarren/bin/</code> launchers you would get from{" "}
    <code>gitwarren service install</code> locally.
  </p>

  <div class="note">
    <span class="note-title">Nothing is left running</span>
    <p>
      GitWarren spawns <code>gitwarren serve --stdio</code> over ssh on demand
      and talks to it down that pipe. There is no daemon sitting on the host
      waiting for you, no port opened, and no service registered unless you ask
      for one. A connection pool hangs up after ten idle minutes.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>Once it is there</h2>

  <p>
    The host&#39;s repositories are added and reviewed exactly as local ones
    are. What travels with a review is the host it belongs to: a link names the
    machine that minted it, so opening review 4 from one install lands on{" "}
    <em>that</em> machine&#39;s review 4 rather than this one&#39;s.
  </p>

  <p>Per-host, you also get:</p>

  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Editors and reveal.</strong> Opening a file from a review on a
      remote host opens it the way that host is configured to, rather than
      guessing a path that only means something locally.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Agent access.</strong> The launcher path to paste into whatever
      agent runs over there, printed for that host rather than for this one.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Attachments.</strong> Images pasted into a comment are ingested on
      the host that owns the review, and served from it.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>When it goes away</h2>

  <p>
    A request that fails tells GitWarren the host has gone, and the screen says
    so rather than showing stale data: a banner, a reconnect with backoff, and a
    refetch when it comes back. There is one honest limit — a host reached over
    ssh has no process of its own to push from, so a machine nobody has asked
    anything in a while is noticed the next time you look at it. Over the
    tailnet it is greyed within a second, because there the socket is held open.
  </p>

  <h2>Removing one</h2>

  <p>
    Removing a host takes it out of this GitWarren&#39;s list. Nothing is
    uninstalled and nothing is deleted: the reviews stay on the machine that
    owns them, and adding it back finds them again.
  </p>
</Docs>
